Give every virtual IP its own Wintun adapter.

0001 installs virtual IPs on the single TUN device kernel-libipsec creates for
itself, which is correct for one tunnel and wrong for two. Windows selects a
source address per *interface*, not per route: with two virtual IPs on one
adapter -- both /32, so no on-link prefix covers either remote network -- it
hands the first address to every destination. The second tunnel's packets then
carry a source matching none of its policies, libipsec drops them, and the
tunnel reports established while carrying nothing.

Measured with two tunnels up (virtual IPs 172.16.17.11 and 10.98.32.91 on one
adapter): `ping 10.98.32.9` lost every packet while `ping -S 10.98.32.91
10.98.32.9` answered in 13ms over the same tunnel, and Find-NetRoute gave
172.16.17.11 as the source for every destination, including the second
tunnel's own subnet.

So create a TUN device per virtual IP and register it with charon, exactly as
kernel_pfroute_net.c does on macOS. kernel_libipsec_router already keys its
devices by virtual IP and hands the matching device's name to install_route(),
so the tunnel's routes follow its address onto its own adapter, and Windows --
which uses the strong host model on send -- has only that adapter's single
address to choose from.

That needs adapters to be distinct, so the fixed adapter name and GUID become
per-slot. Slot 0 keeps the original pair, so the adapter (and the network
profile Windows derived from it) left behind by an earlier run is reused
rather than a new one accumulating on every start. Slots are released on
destroy and reused lowest-first, so names stay stable across connect/
disconnect cycles.

kernel-iph also has to say which of its addresses are virtual. It reported
none ("we currently have no virtual, but regular IPs only"), so
get_address_by_ts() classified a virtual IP as a regular one and
install_route() took the default device anyway -- every tunnel's routes landed
on the idle default adapter while the addresses sat on the per-tunnel ones.
The TUN device list is the record of which addresses are virtual, so the
address enumerator now splits on it.

Registration happens before the address is installed, and add_ip() waits for
the address to appear in the interface list before returning: that list is fed
by NotifyIpInterfaceChange() and is asynchronous, so without the wait the
first CHILD_SA of a connection installs its route before charon can see the
address it belongs to -- and lands on the default device again.
